//? Title: Trial division: safe factors and actual remainder tests
//? Subtitle: Actual native state, retained fixtures and explicit limits
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Factoring a small positive integer requires finding its prime divisors with
multiplicity. Trial division solves this by removing each possible divisor
before advancing, with a square-root worst-case search.
)

\toc

> The method

After removing factors below d, a composite d cannot divide the remainder unless
a smaller prime was missed. Stop when d>n/d: if the remaining n were composite,
it would have a divisor at most its square root. Using n/d avoids overflow in
d*d. For p² with odd prime p, (p−1)/2 odd candidates are visited; remainder
tests also include successful divisions and final failed divisibility checks.

> Simple worked example

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/trial_division_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r = a::trial_division_work(12);
    std::print("| input | actual factors | remainder tests | odd candidates " "|\n|---:|---|---:|---:|\n|12|`{}`|{}|{}|\n",
               r.factors, r.tests, r.odd_candidates);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Positive integer12");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("{} actual remainder tests", r.tests));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Prime factors {}", r.factors));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/trial_division_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| input | actual factors | remainder tests | odd candidates |
|---:|---|---:|---:|
|12|`[2, 2, 3]`|3|0|
